    "result_text": "Full build passes. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nReject creating an owner when the owner's city already holds 50 or more owners, returning HTTP 409.\n\n## Where the logic lives\n\nI reused the existing per-city counting infrastructure (`OwnerRepository.countByCity`, already present and used for customer-code generation) rather than adding new persistence code, and threaded it through the standard layers:\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `countOwnersByCity(String city)`, a thin read-only delegate to `ownerRepository.countByCity(...)`, sitting alongside the other owner-count/existence query methods.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a guard next to the existing conflict checks (duplicate telephone, duplicate household):\n   ```java\n   if (clinicService.countOwnersByCity(owner.getCity()) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY) {\n       return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n   }\n   ```\n   with a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50` constant documenting the threshold.\n\nThe check runs before persisting, so the 51st owner in a city is rejected while the 50th is accepted. This kept each piece in its proper place \u2014 the count query in the repository/service, the HTTP-policy decision in the controller \u2014 without duplicating existing code.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fills a fresh city to exactly 50, expects the 51st to be `409`) passes.\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, all tests green.",
